# 10. Future 29-Agent Integration Compatibility

The eventual system will have 29 dedicated business-category agents.

Each agent must be restricted to its assigned business category and authorized organization context.

This phase must establish which existing database and API structures can support that architecture.

## 10.1 Category Registry Compatibility

Inspect whether existing business categories have:

- Stable identifiers.
- Unique keys or slugs.
- Category metadata.
- Business associations.
- Category-specific settings.
- Category-specific workflows.
- Category-specific permissions.

Document how the existing category IDs map to the established 29-category list.

Do not create the agent registry during this phase.

## 10.2 Agent Context Requirements

Identify the existing mechanisms that could supply an authorized agent with:

- Authenticated user identity.
- Organization or business identity.
- Business category.
- Current location or branch, where applicable.
- User role.
- Relevant products and inventory.
- Purchasing context.
- Sales context.
- Reporting context.
- Subscription and usage permissions.
- Allowed operations.
- Audit and attribution metadata.

Document missing context fields or unresolved identity relationships.

Do not add new fields or tables.

## 10.3 Agent Data Isolation

Future agent execution must not rely solely on instructions inside an AI prompt to enforce security.

Document the existing server-side mechanisms that could enforce:

- Active agent identity.
- Assigned business category.
- Authorized business or organization.
- Tool permissions.
- Resource-level access.
- Tenant-scoped database operations.
- Allowed read/write actions.
- Usage limits.
- Audit logging.

If these controls do not currently exist, record the gap.

## 10.4 Prohibited Cross-Category Access

The future design requires that an agent cannot independently operate outside its assigned category.

For example, a Restaurant agent must not directly execute Pharmacy agent workflows.

Inspect the existing category and authorization architecture for potential support of this restriction.

Shared infrastructure may be reused where appropriate, but business context, tool permissions, and access to tenant data must be enforced for each execution.

Do not implement agents, orchestration, or new permissions during this step.

